WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.810
This video uses a second application to show how a business question is translated into a matrix operation.

2
00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:17.850
As in the café example, here too we first think through the right calculation in business terms, and then develop the matrix operation that matches it.

3
00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.330
The learning goals of this video are the following.

4
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:28.170
First: to translate a business question into a matrix operation.

5
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.178
Second: to recognise which quantities belong in the matrix and which belong in the vector.

6
00:00:36.828 --> 00:00:41.764
And third: to interpret the result of a matrix-vector product in business terms.

7
00:00:44.414 --> 00:00:51.604
Imagine a small ice cream workshop that makes three kinds: soft serve, sundaes and sorbet.

8
00:00:52.204 --> 00:00:55.296
Production runs in batches of five litres each.

9
00:00:57.096 --> 00:01:03.717
Three raw materials are needed for that: milk in litres, fruit in kilograms and chocolate in bars.

10
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:14.901
How much of each raw material one batch needs is shown in this table: the raw materials in the rows, the products in the columns.

11
00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:22.485
One batch of soft serve needs four litres of milk, one kilogram of fruit and two bars of chocolate.

12
00:01:23.035 --> 00:01:29.999
For one batch of sundaes it is five litres of milk, two kilograms of fruit and three bars of chocolate.

13
00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:37.465
The sorbet, on the other hand, needs no milk and no chocolate at all. It only needs six kilograms of fruit.

14
00:01:38.015 --> 00:01:43.803
So the two zeros show that these raw materials do not go into the sorbet at all.

15
00:01:45.703 --> 00:01:55.571
For the coming week, the following production plan is scheduled: ten batches of soft serve, six batches of sundaes and four batches of sorbet.

16
00:01:56.171 --> 00:01:59.460
How much of each raw material is needed in total for that?

17
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:12.853
Let us first work that out in a completely elementary way: for each raw material, the amount needed per batch times the number of batches, summed over the three kinds.

18
00:02:13.453 --> 00:02:17.070
For the milk: four litres times ten batches of soft serve.

19
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.161
Plus five litres times six batches of sundaes.

20
00:02:21.611 --> 00:02:25.709
The sorbet needs no milk, so zero times four.

21
00:02:26.159 --> 00:02:31.560
Together that is forty plus thirty plus zero, so seventy litres of milk.

22
00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:41.480
For the fruit we get: one kilogram times ten, plus two times six, plus six times four. That is forty-six kilograms.

23
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:49.935
And for the chocolate: two times ten, plus three times six, plus zero times four. That is thirty-eight bars.

24
00:02:52.635 --> 00:03:00.867
How can we turn this into a matrix operation? To find out, let us look at the three lines of the calculation more closely.

25
00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:09.266
The production quantities ten, six and four show up again in every line, always the same factors in the same place.

26
00:03:09.866 --> 00:03:16.193
The amounts needed, on the other hand, differ: for each product and each raw material there is a different number.

27
00:03:18.093 --> 00:03:27.396
So the amounts needed depend on two characteristics, on the raw material and on the product. Quantities like these belong in a matrix.

28
00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:32.711
The production quantities depend on only one characteristic, on the product. They form a vector.

29
00:03:34.861 --> 00:03:45.888
We can represent this calculation as a matrix operation if we multiply a matrix holding the amounts needed, on the left, by a vector holding the production quantities, on the right.

30
00:03:46.588 --> 00:03:53.896
The meanings fit together correctly when the columns of the matrix hold the products, in the same order as in the column vector.

31
00:03:54.546 --> 00:04:00.581
The rows of the matrix then stand for the raw materials, and so do the rows of the result.

32
00:04:01.181 --> 00:04:06.165
We call the requirements matrix A, and the production plan x.

33
00:04:08.865 --> 00:04:12.677
Now we compute the product A times x.

34
00:04:13.277 --> 00:04:20.014
As in the introductory video, the column vector is tipped onto its side and laid over the first row of the matrix.

35
00:04:20.614 --> 00:04:27.718
The numbers that sit above one another are multiplied and then added: forty plus thirty plus zero gives seventy.

36
00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:32.784
Seventy litres of milk: that is the first entry of the result.

37
00:04:33.434 --> 00:04:38.739
Now the tipped vector moves down one row. For the fruit we get forty-six.

38
00:04:39.339 --> 00:04:41.768
And in the third row, for the chocolate, thirty-eight.

39
00:04:42.418 --> 00:04:51.486
The result is the raw material requirement for the whole production plan: seventy litres of milk, forty-six kilograms of fruit and thirty-eight bars of chocolate.

40
00:04:54.236 --> 00:05:02.026
Let us compare the first row once more with what we worked out at the start.

41
00:05:02.576 --> 00:05:07.727
It is exactly the same calculation. The matrix-vector product only gathers together what we wrote down separately before.

42
00:05:09.877 --> 00:05:12.889
Briefly, the two checks from the introductory video.

43
00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:21.850
The dimensions: A is three by three, x is three by one. The two inner numbers agree, and the result is three by one.

44
00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:32.888
And the meanings: the columns of A and the rows of x are the products in both cases, while the rows of A and the rows of the result are the raw materials.

45
00:05:36.838 --> 00:05:43.949
Whatever depends on two characteristics, here on the raw material and on the product, belongs in the matrix. The recurring factors form the vector.

46
00:05:45.749 --> 00:05:56.629
The matrix-vector product A times x then gives the raw material requirement of a whole production plan in a single step.

47
00:05:58.429 --> 00:06:05.184
The next step would be to look at several production plans at once. That leads to the product of two matrices.
